# Implement the user-controlled Proton Mail web adapter

## Goal

Provide the primary Free-plan local provider path through Proton Mail's ordinary
web application without requiring Bridge or a third-party direct API identity.

## Required work

- Use a dedicated project browser profile instead of attaching to an existing
  personal browser profile.
- Keep sign-in visible and fully user-controlled; password, second factor,
  CAPTCHA, recovery, and security-key interactions remain outside automation.
- Begin mailbox automation only after the dedicated profile is already signed
  in and the expected Proton Mail application state is visible.
- Never export cookies, local storage, session state, passwords, or challenge
  answers into MCP, config files, fixtures, or logs.
- Implement provider-neutral read, draft, send, reversible mailbox actions, and
  change observation through explicit UI state assertions.
- Prefer semantic roles, labels, and observable application state over brittle
  styling selectors.
- Detect unexpected pages, selector drift, login expiry, and provider changes;
  fail closed instead of clicking a guessed control.
- Require exclusive ownership of the dedicated profile while managed browser
  automation is active. If the profile is locked by the visible login browser,
  fail with a typed retryable state instead of attaching to another profile or
  silently creating a fresh session.
- Keep draft creation and send as distinct actions under the existing
  capability/authority model.

## Acceptance criteria

- A Proton Free account can establish the dedicated profile through visible
  manual login with no Bridge dependency.
- Existing browser profiles are never read or modified.
- Tests prove password and authentication challenge fields are never targeted.
- Read-only workflows cannot trigger mutation or send controls.
- Draft and send remain separately authorized and observable.
- Selector or page-shape drift fails closed before a side effect.
- An opt-in live account can execute every capability advertised by this
  adapter.

## Evidence

- Dedicated-profile lifecycle tests
- Synthetic page-state and selector-drift fixtures
- Read-vs-mutation control boundary tests
- Draft/send side-effect tests
- Redacted opt-in Free-account transcript
## Current implementation

- `protonmail-ai auth login` launches Proton Mail in a fixed, project-owned
  Chromium profile using `--user-data-dir`; it does not attach to a personal
  browser profile or enable remote debugging.
- Absolute XDG data-home resolution, HOME fallback, `0700` Linux permissions,
  executable discovery, and symlink/non-directory rejection are implemented.
- Eleven process tests exercise profile custody and launch behavior with a
  fake browser; active managed automation blocks visible login before browser
  launch, and no live browser/account is used by the deterministic suite.
- `AutomationProfileLease` classifies Chromium `SingletonLock` ownership,
  refuses active/foreign/malformed owners without deleting Chromium state, and
  serializes managed drivers with a `0600` PID/start-time lease, safely reclaims
  dead owners, and protects replacement files by inode/device identity. Ten
  synthetic lease tests cover active, stale, corrupt, symlink,
  local-live/local-stale Chromium, foreign/malformed Chromium, and replacement
  states.
- `mail_web_adapter` owns semantic `UiEvidence`, `UiTarget`, origin binding, and
  action-gate policy. Ten tests prove login/challenge/drift fail closed,
  lookalike origins are rejected, authenticated Mail requires HTTPS
  `mail.proton.me` plus Mail shell evidence, read-only tools cannot target
  mutation/send controls, draft/send remain distinct, destructive capability
  has no target, and every non-destructive capability maps to a semantic target.
- `ManagedBrowser` now launches the dedicated profile with Chromium's private
  DevTools pipe on child FDs 3/4 and no TCP debugging listener. Six synthetic
  driver tests cover handshake, exact provider discovery, lookalike rejection,
  page ambiguity, execution-context origin verification, and origin drift.
- An opt-in smoke with installed Chrome uses only a synthetic profile and proves
  the real FD3/FD4 handshake, target discovery, bounded origin probe, and clean
  `Browser.close` lifecycle without account data.
- `MailShellEvidence` now implements the first semantic page-state extraction.
  After origin revalidation, the driver queries only AX roles `navigation`,
  `search`, `dialog`, and `alertdialog`; it never fetches the full AX tree or
  retains accessible names. `require_mail_shell` fails closed unless navigation
  and search are unignored and no dialog role is visible; it is a snapshot, not
  an action grant, so workflows must revalidate in their own target session.
- Chromium marks its Accessibility CDP domain experimental. `queryAXTree`
  failure is therefore a typed compatibility failure, never a silent selector
  fallback; installed-Chrome/live acceptance must exercise this boundary.
- Eight synthetic shell tests cover ready state, ignored landmarks, dialog and
  alert-dialog blockers, malformed AX output, Account rejection, origin drift
  before and after AX queries, and the typed `MailShellNotReady` workflow
  precondition.
- The pinned WebClients Lumo `open_folder`/`search` handlers are useful semantic
  evidence but are internal: `LumoMailProvider` assembles them from React hooks,
  Redux/store state, router history, and `MailToolDeps`. The web adapter will
  not inject against those private internals; read workflows must use an
  externally observable browser boundary and preserve the same settle/coverage
  semantics.
- The rendered list alone is not sufficient emptiness evidence: WebClients uses
  loading rows in the same list layout, while its internal navigation reads wait
  on loading/context-total state and Encrypted Search completion. External reads
  therefore must prove an observable settle/coverage signal; zero visible rows
  may never be reported as an empty mailbox by itself.
- Content-free mailbox-list inspection now has eight synthetic tests covering
  settled rows, explicit empty, loading, unproven empty, skeleton contradiction,
  duplicate IDs, shell readiness, and post-observation origin drift.
- The first content-bearing read returns a stable snapshot of the currently
  visible page only. Eight synthetic tests cover stable subject/address/unread
  projection, empty subject, explicit empty, loading/unproven state, list
  change, row-order mismatch, and malformed row metadata. `Debug` redacts
  mailbox content and opaque IDs.
- The six WebClients files that define list loading, rows, skeletons, empty
  state, and pagination were byte-identical between the pinned snapshot and
  current `main@4ceca26f3fe59fbf8bcb1b97fe76b298b7c6a586` on 2026-09-30.
- WebClients still exposes no locale-independent general conversation-mode
  marker suitable for Inbox/Archive/etc.; the screen-reader heading reflects the
  mode but is localized, and `NumMessages` is not present on one-message
  conversations. Neither signal is used to guess general list semantics.
- WebClients does, however, force message mode for Drafts, All drafts, Sent, All
  sent, Deleted, and active searches. The relevant `mailSettings.ts`,
  `location.ts`, `elementSearch.ts`, and `mailboxUrl.ts` blobs were rechecked
  byte-identical on current
  `main@4ceca26f3fe59fbf8bcb1b97fe76b298b7c6a586`.
  `MailboxModeEvidence` mirrors those rules
  inside the browser and returns only two booleans, never pathname/hash/search
  content. Three mode-unit tests and five message-page workflow tests prove
  forced/search success, ordinary-Inbox refusal, and before/after mode drift.
- `read_visible_message_page` now wraps stable visible rows only after message
  mode is proven before and after the content snapshot, so its opaque row IDs
  are valid message IDs for that bounded visible-page read.
- `read_next_visible_message_page` now performs one bounded read-only page
  advance using only the verified `pagination-row:go-to-next-page` control. It
  requires explicit page `N`, enabled Next evidence, rechecks the control at
  click time, preserves message mode/origin, waits for settled page `N+1`, and
  then takes another stable snapshot. Seven synthetic tests cover success,
  disabled/control drift, missing current page, unexpected page jump, mode
  drift, and origin drift.
- `MailboxListPagination.tsx` remains byte-identical on current
  `main@4ceca26f3fe59fbf8bcb1b97fe76b298b7c6a586` (blob
  `4f8ffb454bb9769192e48ae0dcba72477f1d317d`), so the locale-independent Next
  contract is current evidence rather than a stale selector guess.
- Visible sort inspection now uses only `filter-dropdown:show-filters`, the four
  stable `toolbar:sort-*` test IDs, and `aria-pressed`; it never selects a sort
  option and restores the menu only when the adapter opened it. Six synthetic
  tests cover newest/oldest proof, pre-opened-menu preservation, contradictory
  sort state, cleanup on failure, and list-message readiness blockers.
- `list_messages` remains deliberately unavailable. The bounded Network
  diagnostic now projects numeric `Message.Time`, which current WebClients uses
  as message receive time, and reconciles it to stable visible IDs. Exact
  provider `Sort=Time&Desc=1` plus visible newest-first still leaves two
  unconditional blockers: equal-Time rows use internal `element.Order` rather
  than ID as tie-break, and visible pagination plus its continuation anchor
  expose no immutable snapshot boundary. A provider sort other than `Time`
  descending adds `ProviderSortKeyUnproven`; non-newest visible state adds
  `SortNotNewestFirst` as a separate blocker.
- `FilterList.tsx`, `filterListHelpers.ts`, `ItemDateRender.tsx`, `date.ts`, and
  `elements.ts` were rechecked byte-identical on current
  `main@e89da53c8a07656184f703383f74250d66f82b1f` on 2026-10-01, so the blockers
  reflect current WebClients behavior.
- `elementQuery.ts` at that same current main commit is also byte-identical
  (blob `63821f797c5c6ba76cf330d82ee2f65ad41ea4e4`). WebClients internally
  chains batches with `Anchor` plus `AnchorID` after the first request, but
  that is a continuation anchor, not evidence of snapshot isolation. `Stale=1`
  is likewise a freshness/refetch signal: `elementsActions.ts` schedules another
  load and `elementsTypes.ts` describes the state as “awaiting fresh data”; no
  immutable boundary or resumable token is exposed. The CDP Network diagnostic
  observes provider list metadata without exporting browser credentials or
  reading private Redux state, so multi-batch collection and snapshot semantics
  remain separate requirements.
- `messages.ts` and `Message.ts` at the same current main commit are also
  byte-identical (blobs `d516591bc008328f25ea01f56a90b9ddf48bbf6e` and
  `9aa1852f264b734f8ab0479e39b32f5ea6df1287`). The ordinary web request is
  `mail/v4/messages`, and its metadata model includes numeric `Time` and
  provider-private `Order`.
- Exact `GET https://mail.proton.me/api/mail/v4/messages` response projection is
  now content-minimizing: it retains only message `ID`, numeric `Time`, and
  provider `Order`, bounds bodies to 256 KiB and pages to 100 rows, rejects the
  same endpoint's draft-creation `POST`, neighboring endpoints, malformed rows,
  and duplicate IDs, and redacts projected metadata from `Debug` output.
- A CDP Network event state machine now scopes itself to one flattened target
  session and retains only request IDs for exact message-list GET requests. It
  bounds unfinished matching requests to 128 entries, requires an exact
  response URL plus HTTP 200 JSON, and accepts only loading-finished order.
  Redirects away, loading failures, and malformed or repeated lifecycle events
  fail closed. Other sessions, mutation traffic, URLs, query terms, request
  headers, cookies, and tokens are not retained.
- The managed driver now wires that state machine into one explicit diagnostic
  observation. It first proves Mail message mode, enables Network with
  `maxPostDataSize=0`, a 256 KiB per-resource buffer, and a 1 MiB total buffer,
  then reloads the same target. Unsolicited CDP events are classified inline and
  discarded rather than queued; the existing 1 MiB frame cap remains in force.
- After an exact list GET reaches HTTP 200 JSON plus `loadingFinished`, the
  driver calls `Network.getResponseBody` only for that request ID. Decoded JSON
  is immediately reduced to message `ID`, numeric `Time`, provider `Order`, and
  `Total`; base64, malformed, oversized, redirected, failed, or ambiguous
  observations fail closed. Network tracking is disabled on both success and
  projection failure before message mode and origin are revalidated.
- Synthetic driver fixtures inject unrelated URLs plus fake Authorization,
  Cookie, and Set-Cookie fields around the matching request. The returned model
  and debug output contain none of them. The fixture proves `Network.disable`
  follows both successful projection and encoded-body rejection.
- A provider-neutral reconciliation model can now combine non-overlapping
  observed list batches against one stable visible message-ID sequence.
  `VisibleMessagePageSnapshot::reconcile_metadata` supplies IDs only from the
  already stable message-mode snapshot, keeps visible order, permits extra
  prefetched provider IDs, and fails closed for duplicate visible IDs,
  overlapping batches, missing visible metadata, or an explicit-empty
  contradiction. Its debug output exposes only row count.
- `observe_visible_message_metadata` now follows the current WebClients
  two-batch query loop; it recognizes only the initial
  `Page`/`PageSize`/`Limit` plus `Sort`/`Desc` shape and the anchored
  continuation shape. Provider sort is reduced to `Time`, `SnoozeTime`, or
  `Size`, direction to one boolean, and both must match the response URL and
  continuation; the capture still retains no full URL and keeps classifying
  Network events
  while fetching batch 1's body so continuation events cannot be discarded.
  Batch 2 is required only when batch 1 fills its limit, and its numeric
  anchor plus bounded anchor ID must equal batch 1's last projected `Time` and
  message ID; the initial zero-based request `Page` is retained as numeric
  metadata and must match the stable visible page's one-based pagination
  evidence before reconciliation; an absent page-number control is accepted
  only for request page zero with no Next control; Network is disabled before a
  fresh stable visible message-page snapshot is reconciled against all captured
  batches; synthetic coverage starts batch 2 during the first body fetch and
  rejects request/visible-page mismatch.
- `inspect_list_messages_readiness` now consumes that sanitized Network
  ordering evidence. Exact `Sort=Time&Desc=1` clears
  `ProviderSortKeyUnproven`; visible newest-first backed by `SnoozeTime` or
  ascending `Time` stays blocked. Synthetic readiness coverage proves both
  `Time`-descending and `SnoozeTime`-descending newest-visible cases. Declared
  `Time` ordering is also checked against projected `Time` plus provider `Order`
  within each batch and at the batch boundary; contradictions fail closed, while
  provider `Order` remains explicitly insufficient for the contract's ID
  tie-break.
- Still required: prove whether the provider anchor can support an immutable
  snapshot boundary and resolve the provider-neutral equal-Time ID tie-break,
  then implement a durable cursor/snapshot chain,
  prove independently safe `list_threads` semantics for grouping-capable
  locations, add composer/mutation workflows and remaining control-level drift
  fixtures, and capture the opted-in Free-account acceptance transcript.
